.TH NCCMP 1

.SH NAME
nccmp \- Compare two NetCDF files

.SH SYNOPSYS
nccmp [OPTION]... FILE1 [FILE2]

.SH DESCRIPTION
nccmp compares two NetCDF files bitwise, semantically or with a user defined tolerance. Comparisons are done in local memory without requiring temporary files. 

.SH OPTIONS
.SS -A, --Attribute=att1[,...] 
Ignore attribute(s) att1[,...] for all variables.

.SS -b, --verbose              
Print messages to show progress.

.SS -B, --buffer-entire-var
Load entire variable into memory to reduce memory allocation runtime overhead.

.SS -c, --var-diff-count=COUNT   
Stop comparing a variable after COUNT data diffs.

.SS -C, --diff-count=COUNT       
Stop comparing after COUNT data diffs total for all variables.

.SS -d, --data  
Compare data (variable values).

.SS -D, --debug
Prints verbose debug messages.

.SS -e, --encoding
Compare these variable encoding settings:
checksum, chunk, deflate, endianness, shuffle, szip

.SS -f, --force  
Forcefully compare, do not stop after first difference.

.SS -F, --fortran   
Print position indices using Fortran style (1-based column major order), instead of C style (0-based row major).

.SS -g, --global
Compare global attributes. (auto enables -m)

.SS -G, --globalex att1[,...]  
Exclude global attributes att1[,...]. (auto enables -g)

.SS -h, --history
Compare global history attribute. (auto enables -g)
  
.SS -H, --help, --usage 
Print usage message.

.SS -i, --info
Print file structure info for one or both files.

.SS -l, --color
Print colored messages.

.SS -m, --metadata
Compare metadata. Use -g to include global attributes.
  
.SS -M, --missing
Ignore difference between values that have different "missing_value" and/or "_FillValue" attributes. Attribute metadata differences are still reported.

.SS -n, --threads=N
Use N POSIX threads. Default is 1.

.SS -N, --nans-are-equal
Allow NaNs to be equal.

.SS -p, --precision=FMT
Global format precision of difference printing. Use '%x' to print bytes as hex string.
.P
FMT defaults differently per data type:
    double '%g'
    float  '%g'
    int    '%d'
    int64  '%lld'
    schar  '0x%02X'
    short  '%d'
    text   '%c'
    uchar  '0x%02X'
    uint   '%d'
    uint64 '%llu'
    ushort '%d'

.SS -P, --header-pad 
Compare header padding in NetCDF3 classic and 64bit file formats.

.SS -q, --quiet
Do not print metadata or data differences.

.SS -r, --group=group1[,...]
Compare group(s) group1[,...] only. Group names can be full or short.
"/" is the file root group. "/group1/subgroup2" is an example fullname, whereas "group1" and "subgroup2" are short names.

.SS -R, --no-subgroups
Do not compare nested groups.

.SS -s, --report-identical-files
Report when files are the same.

.SS -S, --statistics
Report data difference statistics summary table after all comparisons (FILE1 - FILE2).

.SS -t, --tolerance=TOL
Compare if absolute difference > TOL.

.SS -T, --Tolerance=TOL
Compare if relative percent difference > TOL.

.SS -v, --variable=var1[,...]
Compare variable(s) var1[,...] only.

.SS -V, --version
Print program version.

.SS -x, --exclude=var1[,...]
Exclude variable(s) var1[,...].

.SS -w, --warn=tag[,...] 
Selectively make certain differences exceptions, but still print messages. As warnings, these differences will not return exit code 1.
.P
Supported tags and their meanings:
    all:    All differences.
    format: File formats.
    eos:    Extra attribute end-of-string nulls.

.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Print file structure info
$ nccmp -i encoded.nc

.SS Compare metadata and exclude specific variables
$ nccmp -mf -x recdata,rec test1a.nc test1b.nc

.SS Compare data and allow differences up to a threshold
$ nccmp -df -t 1e-3 test4a.nc test4b.nc

.SS Run in parallel and report table up to 5 differences per variable
$ nccmp -df -S --threads 4 -c 5 in1.nc in2.nc

